Tests
=====

None of these need the board.

C++ (firmware logic, run on the laptop with PlatformIO's native platform + Unity)
    pio test -e native                  all suites
    pio test -e native -f test_vm       one suite

    test_vm            expression VM: validator, every opcode, guarded maths, limits (src/expr_vm.cpp)
    test_strip_config  LED strip length / direction clamping (src/strip_config.h)
    test_api_guard     API token comparison and the allow/refuse rule (src/api_guard_logic.h)
    test_audio         microphone maths: dB -> 0..1, band means, smoothing, tuning clamps (src/audio_math.h)

    The logic under test lives in headers/files without Arduino or FreeRTOS includes, so it compiles
    on the laptop. The thin hardware wrappers (api_guard.cpp, the I2S capture) are not covered here.

JavaScript (web page and laptop server)
    npm install                         once: installs jsdom (a fake browser, development only)
    node test/js/run_all.js             everything below, files in parallel (also: npm test), about 12 s
    node test/js/test_server.js         one file

    test_compiler / test_differential   compiler.js, and its results against the firmware VM (needs g++)
    test_firmware_sync                  opcode table, ABI, default pattern, program fingerprint vs the firmware
    test_presets / test_fastled         presets.js and fastled.js
    test_index_html                     structure of the page; slotHash() and presetsOnBoard()
    test_server                         server/server.js, started for real on free ports
    test_page_*                         the real index.html run in jsdom (see dom_harness.js):
                                          token  - API token wrapper, dialog, pairing link
                                          core   - tabs, idle sleep, Custom tab, preset upload, laptop mode
                                          tabs   - Patterns, Basic (wheel, white), Home, power, brightness
                                          setup  - strips, mic tuning, Wi-Fi, diagnostics

    dom_harness.js replaces fetch, timers and canvas with fakes: every request is recorded and answered
    by a route table, and page.tick(ms) moves a virtual clock, so nothing waits in real time.
    Not covered: real drawing and layout (jsdom has neither); that needs a real browser.
